THIRD-PARTY NOTICES
===================

mq-bridge-redpanda ships two artifacts that are distributed together:

  libmq_bridge_redpanda      (Rust cdylib)
  libmq_bridge_redpanda_go   (Go c-shared library)

Both statically link third-party code. This file lists that code and its
license. mq-bridge-redpanda's own source is licensed MIT OR Apache-2.0; see
LICENSE-MIT and LICENSE-APACHE. Choosing an arm of that dual license covers
this project's own code only — everything listed below keeps its own terms.

Scope
-----

The audit unit is the *built artifact*, not go.mod or Cargo.toml. Go build
tags and Cargo features change the linked set, so this file is generated from
`go list -deps` over go-bridge and `cargo metadata` over the plugin crate, at
the versions pinned in go.sum and Cargo.lock. Regenerate it with:

    python3 scripts/gen-third-party-notices.py

Anything that changes the curated component imports in go-bridge/main.go, the
Cargo feature set, or a pinned version invalidates this file.

The Go sibling is built with an explicit component allowlist, not the
`public/bundle/free` aggregate. That is a licensing decision as much as a size
one: the free bundle reaches packages under `public/components/community`
(`cohere`, `openai`, `ollama`) whose Redpanda Community License headers sit on
top of Apache-2.0 implementation files. None of those are linked here.

Redpanda Connect dual-license check
-----------------------------------

Redpanda Connect is dual-licensed: Apache-2.0 for the majority of connectors,
and the Redpanda Community License (RCL) for enterprise features, applied by a
per-file header. Every Connect source file linked into this build carries the
Apache-2.0 header; none carries the RCL header. Re-verify after changing the
component allowlist:

    cd go-bridge
    grep -rl "Redpanda Community" $(go list -deps -f '{{{{.Dir}}}}' . | grep connect/v4)

which must print nothing (grep exits 1).

Mozilla Public License 2.0 obligations
--------------------------------------

Some linked Go modules are MPL-2.0 (marked below). MPL-2.0 is file-level
copyleft: distributing a binary that includes them requires making the source
of *those files* available to recipients, under MPL-2.0, and saying where. The
unmodified upstream sources at the pinned versions satisfy this. If you fork
or patch an MPL-2.0 module, you must publish the modified files.

